---
/**
 * The state park rule, in full.
 *
 * The whole page turns on one distinction that Delaware's own summary sentence
 * makes easy to miss: the ban is on GUARDED SWIMMING BEACHES, not on state
 * park beaches. Everything else follows from that.
 *
 * Also carries the 2026 fee increase, which most published guides have not
 * caught up with — beach parks doubled.
 */
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FlagIcon from "../../components/FlagIcon.astro";

import placesData from "../../data/places.json";
import { assertAnswerLength, isPublishable, robotsFor } from "../../lib/verification";

const parks = (placesData as any[]).find((p) => p.slug === "state-park-surf-fishing-beaches");
const pageGate = {
  verifiedDate: isPublishable(parks) ? "confirmed" : null,
  verifiedSource: isPublishable(parks) ? "confirmed" : null,
};

const answer =
  "Only on the guarded swimming beaches, and only from May 1 to September 30. Everywhere else in the park — the non-guarded sand and the drive-on surf-fishing beaches — leashed dogs are legal all year, including July.";

assertAnswerLength("state-parks", answer);

const fees = [
  { label: "Beach parks, in-state vehicle", value: "$10 / day" },
  { label: "Beach parks, out-of-state", value: "$20 / day" },
  { label: "Inland parks, in-state", value: "$5 / day" },
  { label: "Inland parks, out-of-state", value: "$10 / day" },
  { label: "Annual pass, in-state", value: "$50" },
  { label: "Annual pass, out-of-state", value: "$100" },
];

const closed = [
  "Guarded swimming beaches, May 1 – September 30",
  "Beach Plum Island — closed to pets year-round",
  "Shorebird nesting areas",
  "Surfboarding and sailboarding beaches",
];

const schema = isPublishable(parks)
  ? [
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        mainEntity: [
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            name: "Are dogs allowed in Delaware State Parks?",
            acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: answer },
          },
        ],
      },
    ]
  : [];
---

<Layout
  title="Are dogs allowed in Delaware State Parks? Rules and fees"
  description="Delaware bans dogs from guarded swimming beaches May 1 – September 30 only. Non-guarded and surf-fishing sand takes leashed dogs all year. With 2026 fees."
  robots={robotsFor(pageGate)}
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-6">
    <p class="label m-0">State parks</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.5rem] sm:text-[3.3rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[15ch] text-marsh">
      Are dogs banned from Delaware State Parks in summer?
    </h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field">
    <p
      class="text-[1.28rem] leading-[1.45] font-medium max-w-[54ch] border-l-[5px] border-l-ochre ps-4 sm:ps-5"
    >
      {answer}
    </p>

    <h2>The distinction the whole thing turns on</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      State law prohibits pets from <strong>lifeguarded beaches</strong> between
      May 1 and September 30. It is very easy to read that as "dogs are banned
      from the state park beaches in summer," and almost every guide does. But a
      guarded swimming beach is a small marked stretch of a very long park. The
      rest of the sand — the non-guarded portions, and the drive-on surf-fishing
      beaches at Cape Henlopen, Delaware Seashore and Fenwick Island — is open
      to leashed dogs every day of the year.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      That is the entire answer to the summer question, and it is sitting inside
      the sentence everyone quotes as a ban.
    </p>


    <h2>Where dogs are closed out</h2>
    <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 my-5">
      {
        closed.map((c) => (
          <li class="flex gap-2.5 items-start py-2 border-b border-tide/30">
            <FlagIcon icon="cross" class="w-[1em] h-[1em] mt-1 shrink-0 text-flag-red" />
            <span class="text-[0.95rem] leading-snug">{c}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2>Conditions everywhere else</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Leashed at all times.</strong> Six feet is the statewide maximum outside designated off-leash areas.</li>
      <li><strong>Proof of rabies vaccination is required</strong> in state parks. Carry the tag or the paperwork.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean up.</strong> Use the doggie dooleys where they exist; carry bags where they don't.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it costs in 2026</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Entry fees rose on 1 March 2026 and the beach parks roughly doubled. Most
      guides still quote the old numbers, so budget from this table instead of
      from a blog post.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap pb-4">
    <dl class="sign breakout my-6 divide-y-2 divide-tide/30">
      {
        fees.map((f) => (
          <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-3 flex items-baseline justify-between gap-6">
            <dt class="label">{f.label}</dt>
            <dd class="m-0 stamp text-[0.95rem] text-ink shrink-0">{f.value}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p class="stamp text-ink-soft mt-0 mb-0">
      Entry charged 1 March – 30 November. Surf fishing permits are priced
      separately and were unchanged for 2026.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <h2>The tradeoff nobody mentions</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The beaches where your dog is legal in summer are the beaches without
      lifeguards — which means the ones people drive onto and fish from.
      Discarded fishhooks and cut monofilament line are the genuine hazard here,
      and a hook in a paw or a swallowed line is a vet trip, not an
      inconvenience. Walk in front of the dog on a short leash near the tackle
      line, and don't let it forage in the wrack.
    </p>

    <h2>Lums Pond is the other one to know</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Lums Pond State Park has the highest-rated dog area in Delaware and two
      facts almost nobody publishes together: it is <strong>not fenced</strong>,
      and it charges park entry to get in. It is also a millpond, which matters
      in August — see <a href="/dogs/pond-safety/">pond safety</a> before letting
      a dog swim there.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/dogs/summer/">Where a dog is legal in July — all five options</a></li>
      <li><a href="/dogs/dog-parks/">Delaware dog parks and off-leash areas, on eight fields</a></li>
      <li><a href="/dogs/">Beach rules by town</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
